+
+## Example
+
+Take one unbroken compact U(1) gauge factor with two left-chiral superfields of charges $+1$ and $-1$, one of each and no additional multiplicities. Use the canonical matter kinetic term with no superpotential ($F=0$) or additional real interactions ($G=0$), and a formal perturbative Wilsonian regulator satisfying the theorem at scale $\lambda>0$. Let the bare field-independent Fayet–Iliopoulos coefficient be $\xi$ in the term $\xi\int d^4x\,d^4\theta\,V_1$. Then the perturbative Wilsonian coefficient satisfies $\xi_\lambda=\xi$ at every loop order: the one-loop correction cancels by the charge sum, and the theorem excludes higher-loop corrections. Other real D-term couplings, if added, are not protected by this conclusion.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Weinberg, the U(1) Fayet–Iliopoulos argument, pp. 7–8](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9803099v1)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The N=1 Wilsonian action datum, an unbroken U(1) factor, its two left-chiral representations, and the formal perturbative regulator hypotheses.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-wilsonian-n1-action-data]]: for a U(1) factor, $V_1$ is a real Abelian prepotential and a constant coefficient $\xi$ defines the Fayet–Iliopoulos D-term.
+
+[F2] [[pthm-qftx-n1-wilsonian-f-term-nonrenormalization]]: the perturbative FI correction is one-loop only, is proportional to the sum of all left-chiral U(1) charges, and vanishes when this sum is zero.
+
+[A1] The two multiplets have charges $q_+=1$ and $q_-=-1$, each with multiplicity one.
+
+[A2] The U(1) background is unbroken, $F=G=0$, and the regulator and measure preserve the gauge and rigid N=1 Ward identities.
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 The total left-chiral charge is $q_++q_-=1+(-1)=0$. [A1, arithmetic]
+
+1.2 By [F2], the only possible perturbative FI correction is the one-loop tadpole proportional to this total charge, so it is zero; all higher-loop corrections are excluded. Therefore $\xi_\lambda=\xi$. The theorem makes no corresponding claim about other D terms. [F1, F2, A2, step 1.1] ∎
+
